| 1 | K | Beat, high and low. *What can we hear in music, and what does music go with?* | 18 | mu-k-read; mu-k-respond | MU:Pr4.2.Ka; MU:Re7.2.Ka; MU:Re9.1.Ka; MU:Cn11.1.Ka | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: Camille Saint-SaA�ns, The Carnival of the Animals (France, 1886), "The Elephant" (low and slow) and "Aviary" (high and quick), a public domain score in a recording whose rights line the unit plan checks; the dA1ndA�n talking drum of the Yoruba people (Nigeria), squeezed to make high and low tones, heard on a museum's or a sound archive's page, described and linked; "Kye Kye Kule", a traditional call-and-response game song taught as from Ghana, sung by the class | MU:Pr4.2.Ka: notice high and low, loud and soft, and same and different in the songs the class sings, and show each with the body (hands up and down, big and small moves, a same-or-different sign). MU:Re7.2.Ka: show how a music idea is used while a piece plays: pat the beat, and trace the tune going up or down with a hand. MU:Re9.1.Ka: judge a piece by taste and how it sounds ("I like it because it is soft"). MU:Cn11.1.Ka: show how music goes with other arts, a story, a game or a time of day (the elephant music and an elephant picture; the game song and the playground). Technique: the steady beat patted on the knees and walked in place; the singing voice found by a soft siren slide, never forced. Words: beat, high, low, loud, soft, same. Showing: "Kye Kye Kule" sung and played as a call-and-response game for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.2.Ka, a table item: the teacher sings three short pairs (high or low, loud or soft, same or different) and the child shows each with the body; MU:Re7.2.Ka, a performance item: while an unseen Curio Class original plays, the child pats eight steady beats, then traces the tune up or down; MU:Re9.1.Ka, a talk item: likes the unseen piece or not, with one thing about its sound; MU:Cn11.1.Ka, a talk item: from three picture cards (a story, a game, bedtime) the child picks the one the piece goes with and says why. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: any notation (grade 1's picture symbols), tonality and meter (grade 2). Tools: step cards for the beat and the siren slide, picture cards and work cards, Curio Class's own; a beat practice tool on the teacher screen or the printed beat strip of hearts. Free path: knees and hands as the drum; the teacher sings when no device is there. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First because every later unit needs the steady beat, the singing voice, the listening routine and the sound rules (the rhythm-syllables and describe-interpret-judge models start here). Listening opens the year so a child hears music before making it up. Longest kindergarten unit: four ids, two techniques and the set-up, circle and sound routines learned for the first time | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on the set-up minutes when 30 children get sticks or shakers in a 40-minute session | Hazards and controls: moving to the beat in a cleared circle with arms out, stepping in place, no running; recordings at a set low level the class can talk over; no shouting, the siren slide soft and short, no child sings alone; rhythm sticks tapped on the floor or each other, never near a face. Talking drum: the sensitivity agent reads the holding museum's or archive's page and a Yoruba drummer's own published words first; the class listens only and does not imitate the drum's speech. Kye Kye Kule: the agent checks the song's published history and that it is a game song, not a sacred one. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | K | Loud and soft, fast and slow. *How do singers and players show what a song means, and which music do we like?* | 18 | mu-k-express; mu-k-choose | MU:Pr4.3.Ka; MU:Re8.1.Ka; MU:Pr4.1.Ka; MU:Re7.1.Ka; MU:Cn10.1.Ka | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re8); connecting (Cn10). Works: Edvard Grieg, "In the Hall of the Mountain King" from Peer Gynt (Norway, 1875), a tune that grows louder and faster, a public domain score in a checked recording; San Jose Taiko (San Jose, California, founded 1973; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), a taiko ensemble whose pieces move from very soft to very loud, described and linked to the group's page; "ArrorrA3 mi niA�o", a traditional lullaby of Spain and Latin America, sung by the class | MU:Pr4.3.Ka: sing one song loud and soft, fast and slow, and notice which way fits what its words mean (the lullaby soft and slow). MU:Re8.1.Ka: hear a performer get louder or faster and point out where the change comes and what it shows (the mountain king growing; a taiko piece from soft to loud). MU:Pr4.1.Ka: show and say what interests the child in a piece, from a class menu of three short songs. MU:Re7.1.Ka: list the child's interests (a picture list: animals, trains, dancing, sleeping) and show why some music is a favorite. MU:Cn10.1.Ka: show how what the child likes or knows shaped a choice in performing ("I chose soft because ___"), with a story character's choice ("what would the sleepy bear choose?") offered beside it and marked the same. Technique: a soft voice and a strong voice without shouting (the "small voice, big voice" card); a tempo change in the feet (slow steps, quick tiptoes). The listening log begins. Words: loud, soft, fast, slow, favorite, choose. Showing: each group sings the lullaby and a choose-your-way song in the way the group picked, for the partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.3.Ka, a performance item: the group sings a short song soft, then loud, and each child points to the picture of the way that fits the words; MU:Re8.1.Ka, a talk item: in an unseen Curio Class original that changes, the child shows where it got louder or faster and says what it feels like; MU:Pr4.1.Ka, a talk item: the child picks one of three short songs heard and shows or says what is interesting in it; MU:Re7.1.Ka, a kept listening-log page made on the last making day: the picture list of interests and the circled favorite piece, with a dictated reason; MU:Cn10.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the showing: the way the child chose for the song and the "because" that ties it to what the child (or the story character) likes or knows. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: naming how loudness and speed carry meaning (grade 1), tone color and other qualities (grade 2). Tools: the "small voice, big voice" card, picture menus and the first listening-log pages, Curio Class's own. Free path: the teacher sings the menu songs. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | After unit 1 because loud and soft and fast and slow are heard against the steady beat and sung with the singing voice found there; before unit 3 because motifs are made with loudness and speed as choices, and the habit of saying why a choice was made starts here. The expression-map and listening-log models start here | Candidate: a lullaby from an Asian or Native American community, from that community's own published teaching, beside ArrorrA3 | Hazards and controls: taiko and the mountain king played at a set low level, never turned up for effect; a child who is sensitive to noise may cover the ears, wear hearing protection or sit at the far side; a quiet rest after the loud pieces; the big voice is strong, never a shout. Interests: the picture list is the child's own or a story character's, marked the same; no question about home or family music. San Jose Taiko: the sensitivity agent reads the group's own published history first (taiko ensembles grew in Japanese American communities); no costume, and the class does not play on a taiko. The lullaby is sung as a song; no role play. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | K | Making up music. *How do musicians make up a new idea, and how can we keep it?* | 10 | mu-k-invent; mu-k-document | MU:Cr1.1.Ka; MU:Cr1.1.Kb; MU:Cr2.1.Ka | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Ludwig van Beethoven, Symphony No. 5, its opening (Germany, 1808), a short motif of four sounds that returns, a public domain score in a checked recording; Bobby McFerrin (United States, born 1950; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), voice-and-body improvising, described and linked to the artist's page | MU:Cr1.1.Ka: explore beat and the shape of a tune by moving, singing and tapping (a "follow my hands" game: the voice rises when the hands rise). MU:Cr1.1.Kb: invent a short motif of three or four sounds from a movement (a hop becomes "pa, pa, PA"). MU:Cr2.1.Ka: pick a favorite motif from the class game and keep it, in order, as a row of picture symbols (big and small dots for loud and soft, placed high and low on the card) or a sound-only recording on the class device; both the pick and the keeping are taught and checked. The idea bank and the first notation-ladder step begin. Words: motif, idea, pick, keep, symbol. Showing: the idea bank played as a chain, each group's motif in turn, for the class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.Ka, a performance item: the child follows the teacher's tune-shape hands with the voice for a short slide, then pats a steady beat under a short tune; MU:Cr1.1.Kb, a performance item: from a movement card the child makes a motif and plays it twice the same; MU:Cr2.1.Ka, a kept card made on the last making day: the chosen motif in picture symbols (or the class recording log line), with the child saying which motif was the favorite, scored with the showing. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a purpose for an idea and tonality and meter (grade 1). Tools: movement cards, picture-symbol cards, the idea-bank tub, Curio Class's own. Free path: hands, feet and voice; picture symbols in crayon. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | After units 1 and 2 because motifs are made from the beat, high and low, and loud and soft, and the child picks among them as unit 2 taught; before unit 4 because the concert polishes a motif from the idea bank. The idea-bank and notation-ladder models start here. Shortest kindergarten unit: three ids, one technique | Candidate: a motif-built piece by a living maker from Asia or Africa, described and linked | Hazards and controls: shaker bottles filled and taped shut by an adult, no small loose parts, no glass; no mouth instrument shared; movement cards are stepped in place in the cleared circle. McFerrin is taught through his own pages. A recording is of the sound only and stays on the school's device (APPROACH "Kept work"). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | K | Our first concert. *How do musicians make their music better, and how do we play for an audience?* | 14 | mu-k-revise; mu-k-perform | MU:Cr3.1.Ka; MU:Cr3.2.Ka; MU:Pr5.1.Ka; MU:Pr5.1.Kb; MU:Pr6.1.Ka; MU:Pr6.1.Kb | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Ella Jenkins (United States, born 1924; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), "You'll Sing a Song and I'll Sing a Song", her call-and-response singing with children, recorded for a folk music archive's label, described and linked to the archive's page; San Jose Taiko from unit 2, revisited: how an ensemble practices and plays to an audience, from the group's own page | MU:Cr3.1.Ka: with help from self, a partner and the teacher, change one thing in the group's motif (louder, slower, one sound added), by the critique routine (describe what you hear, ask, suggest). MU:Cr3.2.Ka: play the finished motif for classmates. MU:Pr5.1.Ka: use feedback from self, teacher and peers to improve a song performance. MU:Pr5.1.Kb: try suggested ways to practice that make the song more expressive (echo it in parts, slow then up to speed, sing it with the hands only). MU:Pr6.1.Ka: perform the song expressively, with its planned soft and loud parts. MU:Pr6.1.Kb: perform in a way that suits the audience (face the audience, wait for quiet, start and stop together, bow). The practice-plan and critique-protocol models begin. Words: practice, again, feedback, finished, audience, bow. Showing: the class concert for families or a partner class on the unit's last day, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (the class concert in groups on the check day), id by id: MU:Cr3.1.Ka, a talk item right after the group's turn: the one change the group made and who suggested it (checked against the record sheet from the critique day); MU:Cr3.2.Ka, a guide look-for: the finished motif played for the class; MU:Pr5.1.Ka, a guide look-for: the part named in the group's feedback is better in the check take than on the record sheet's rehearsal mark; MU:Pr5.1.Kb, a table item: the child shows one practice way (echo or slow then fast) on a short phrase when asked; MU:Pr6.1.Ka, a guide look-for: the song sung with its planned soft and loud parts; MU:Pr6.1.Kb, a guide look-for: audience manners (faces the class, starts and stops with the group, bows). Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a rehearsal strategy on a hard spot (grade 1), set criteria (grade 2). Tools: the practice card, the critique card in pictures, the concert plan card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the class's own songs, found instruments. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in the year because the concert is built from the year's songs and the idea bank's motifs (units 1 to 3), and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the unit's check day, the class concert in groups, is its own end-of-unit assessment, and the family concert follows it | Candidate: a doubled version of every group part for the home room (parent and child sing it) | No child sings alone: groups of five, and a child who will not sing may play a found-sound part or hold the group's picture card while singing quietly, marked on the same look-fors; a shy child stands in the middle of the group. Stage nerves are named as normal, and the respond day is a second try. The concert's audience is invited with the family's yes, and any recording is sound only on the school's device. Hazards and controls: volume and voice rules as unit 1; the performing space cleared, groups stand, no steps or risers for K. Ella Jenkins: the sensitivity agent reads the archive's page and her own published words first. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 1 | Beat and tune around the world. *What jobs do beat and tune do in music from different places?* | 16 | mu-g1-read; mu-g1-respond | MU:Pr4.2.1a; MU:Pr4.2.1b; MU:Re7.2.1a; MU:Re9.1.1a; MU:Cn11.1.1a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: John Philip Sousa, "The Stars and Stripes Forever" (United States, 1896), in a United States Marine Band recording, a federal work in the public domain, a march whose beat is for marching; R. Carlos Nakai (Navajo and Ute, born 1946; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a flute piece from his album Canyon Trilogy (1989), the Native American flute's tune rising and falling, described and linked to the artist's or the label's page; "Sakura Sakura" (Japan, a traditional song of the Edo period, public domain), sung by the class | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten contrasts shown with the body (mu-k-read) and liking a piece with a reason (mu-k-respond). MU:Pr4.2.1a: show the beat and the way a melody moves (up, down, same) in music from many cultures (the three works and the class songs). MU:Pr4.2.1b: read and perform rhythm patterns from picture notation (a row of four hearts, one picture for "ta", two for "ti-ti") while learning the class songs; beat counts and picture symbols only, no note values (lesson 13). MU:Re7.2.1a: show how beat or pitch serves a purpose in different styles (the march's beat for feet; the flute's slow rise for calm). MU:Re9.1.1a: judge a piece for a purpose by taste and expression ("good for marching because ___"). MU:Cn11.1.1a: show how a piece relates to another art, a school subject, a place or daily life (the march and a parade picture; Sakura and the cherry trees of a spring picture). Words: beat, melody, up, down, ta, ti-ti, purpose. Showing: rhythm patterns read and played on found percussion with "Sakura Sakura" for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.2.1a, a performance item: during an unseen open recording from a culture not studied, the child pats the beat, then shows the melody's direction with a hand; MU:Pr4.2.1b, a performance item: the child reads and claps a four-beat picture card; MU:Re7.2.1a, a talk item: for an unseen piece, the child shows the beat or the tune and says what it is for; MU:Re9.1.1a, a kept listening-log page made on the last making day: of two unseen short pieces, the one circled as better for a named purpose (a lullaby or a march), with a dictated reason; MU:Cn11.1.1a, a talk item: the story, place, art or school subject a piece goes with, and why. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tonality and meter named (grade 2), melody patterns read (grade 2). Tools: picture-notation cards, the beat strip, work cards, Curio Class's own; a picture-notation reader on the teacher screen or the printed cards. Free path: found percussion, the teacher sings. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 1 because it reopens the beat and the listening routine after the summer and starts reading, so unit 7 can write ideas in the same picture symbols. Both kindergarten models (rhythm-syllables, describe-interpret-judge) are retrieved here, where they are first needed this year | Candidate: a fourth piece from Africa or South America with a clear beat for a purpose, in an open recording | Nakai: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own published words and a Native-run source on the flute first; the piece is his own concert music, not a ceremonial song; the class listens only, with no Native flute imitation and no costume. Sakura: sung in Japanese from a read-aloud guide checked against a Japanese-language source; no costume. The march is taught as a parade's beat; no war story. Hazards and controls: marching in place in the cleared space; found percussion with no small parts and no glass, played at a hand-pat level. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 1 | Two ways to sing it. *How do loudness and speed change what a song means, and how do we pick music for a purpose?* | 16 | mu-g1-express; mu-g1-choose | MU:Pr4.3.1a; MU:Re8.1.1a; MU:Pr4.1.1a; MU:Re7.1.1a; MU:Cn10.1.1a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re8); connecting (Cn10). Works: Johannes Brahms, "Wiegenlied" (Lullaby), Op. 49 No. 4 (Germany, 1868), a public domain score in a checked recording; Ladysmith Black Mambazo (South Africa, a men's choir founded by Joseph Shabalala in the 1960s; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), singing isicathamiya, a soft, close style, described and linked to the group's page; Sousa's march from unit 5, revisited beside the lullaby | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten loud, soft, fast and slow (mu-k-express) and the favorites page (mu-k-choose). MU:Pr4.3.1a: show and describe how music expresses, through loudness and speed, by singing one song two ways. MU:Re8.1.1a: name how loudness and speed show what a creator or performer means (the choice a recorded performer made). MU:Pr4.1.1a: show and discuss interest in varied music and its purpose (a pick from a menu of four for a purpose: a lullaby, a march, a game). MU:Re7.1.1a: show how the child's interests and experiences shape a music choice for a purpose. MU:Cn10.1.1a: show how what the child likes, knows or can do shaped a choice in performing (the way the group sings its song), with a public or story-character option marked the same. Words: loudness, speed, express, creator, performer, purpose. Showing: each group sings one song in the way it chose for its purpose and says why, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.3.1a, a performance item: the group sings a short song two ways, and the child says which way fits the song's meaning; MU:Re8.1.1a, a talk item: the loudness or speed choice in an unseen open recording and what it shows; MU:Pr4.1.1a, a kept listening-log page made on the last making day: the piece picked from the menu for a purpose, with a dictated reason; MU:Re7.1.1a, a talk item: why this pick, tied to something the child likes or has heard (or a story character's), marked the same; MU:Cn10.1.1a, a guide look-for on the showing: the group's chosen way, with the child's "we chose ___ because ___". Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: qualities beyond loudness and speed (grade 2), comparing two performers (grade 2). Tools: the expression map, menu cards, listening-log pages, Curio Class's own. Free path: the teacher sings the menu songs. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because the purpose that beat and pitch serve grows into loudness and speed chosen for a purpose; before unit 7 because a reply phrase made for a purpose uses these choices. The expression-map and listening-log models are retrieved here | Candidate: a lullaby or soft song by a living maker from Asia or the Americas, described and linked | Choices: the row's "music the child hears at home" is offered beside a public option (music at school, on the street, in a story), marked the same; no child explains a family's music or culture. Ladysmith Black Mambazo: the sensitivity agent reads the group's own pages and a museum's or archive's page on isicathamiya first (it grew among Zulu migrant workers); the class listens only and does not imitate the style's stage steps. Hazards and controls: recordings at the set low level; voice rules as unit 1. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 1 | Reply phrases. *How do musicians answer a musical question, and how can we write our answer down?* | 14 | mu-g1-invent; mu-g1-document | MU:Cr1.1.1a; MU:Cr1.1.1b; MU:Cr2.1.1a; MU:Cr2.1.1b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Johann Strauss II, "The Blue Danube" (Austria, 1866), a waltz that moves in threes, a public domain score in a checked recording; Gustav Mahler, Symphony No. 1, third movement (Austria and Hungary, first played 1889), where the round "FrA"re Jacques" is played slow and in minor, a public domain score in a checked recording; the class's own "FrA"re Jacques" sung in major beside it | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten motifs (mu-k-invent) and picture symbols (mu-k-document). MU:Cr1.1.1a: with little help, make a reply phrase for a purpose (a greeting reply, a goodnight reply) to the teacher's sung call. MU:Cr1.1.1b: with little help, invent ideas that sound happy or sad (major or minor, the teacher's tune sung or played both ways) and that move in twos (a march) or threes (a waltz), counted "1, 2" or "1, 2, 3" with no fractions. MU:Cr2.1.1a: play chosen ideas and say why they were chosen to express what the child meant. MU:Cr2.1.1b: document the reply in picture notation (unit 5's hearts and ta and ti-ti pictures, high and low placed on the card) or a sound-only recording. Words: reply, phrase, happy, sad, twos, threes. Showing: greeting and goodnight replies played as a call-and-reply chain for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.1a, a performance item: the child answers the teacher's call with a reply for a named purpose; MU:Cr1.1.1b, a performance item: one idea played in twos and then in threes, or happy and then sad, with the teacher's accompaniment; MU:Cr2.1.1a, a talk item: why this reply fits its purpose; MU:Cr2.1.1b, a kept card made on the last making day: the reply in picture notation, or the recording log line, scored with the showing. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: improvising inside a given tonality and meter (grade 2), combining ideas in order (grade 2). Tools: call cards, picture-notation cards, a free on-screen keyboard (a Curio Class tool) or the sung happy and sad versions. Free path: voice and found percussion; notation in crayon. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 5, whose picture notation the child now writes with, and unit 6, whose choices for a purpose a reply phrase makes; before unit 8 because the concert polishes a reply phrase from the idea bank | Candidate: a call-and-response song from a West African or African American tradition, from that community's own published teaching, as a third work | Mahler's movement is often told as a funeral march: for grade 1 the unit says "slow and sad" and tells no story of death, and the sensitivity agent reads the published program note first. Hazards and controls: found percussion as unit 5; voice rules as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | 1 | Songs for the day. *How do musicians practice a hard part, and how do we fit a song to its audience?* | 14 | mu-g1-revise; mu-g1-perform | MU:Cr3.1.1a; MU:Cr3.2.1a; MU:Pr5.1.1a; MU:Pr5.1.1b; MU:Pr6.1.1a; MU:Pr6.1.1b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Mariachi Los Camperos (Los Angeles, United States, founded by Natividad Cano in 1961; the unit plan records whether active, as of its date), a mariachi ensemble's performance described and linked to the group's or its label's page: how an ensemble fits its playing to the occasion; "Los pollitos dicen" (a traditional children's song of Spanish-speaking countries), sung by the class; Ladysmith Black Mambazo from unit 6, revisited: how a choir blends after practice | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten revising with help (mu-k-revise) and the practice card (mu-k-perform). MU:Cr3.1.1a: polish a reply phrase from the idea bank with self, partner and teacher feedback (the critique routine). MU:Cr3.2.1a: share the finished music with classmates, showing what it is for and what it means. MU:Pr5.1.1a: use feedback from self, teacher and peers to improve a song performance. MU:Pr5.1.1b: try a suggested rehearsal strategy on a hard spot (slow then up to speed, echo in parts, mouth the words). MU:Pr6.1.1a: perform expressively for a purpose (a good-morning song for the school, a goodnight song for families). MU:Pr6.1.1b: perform in a way that fits both the audience and the purpose (a gentle start for younger children, a bright start for a morning song). Words: rehearse, strategy, polish, purpose, audience, expressive. Showing: a "songs for the day" concert for families or a younger class on the unit's last day, with the family's yes. End-of-unit evidence (the class concert in groups on the check day), id by id: MU:Cr3.1.1a, a talk item right after the group's turn: what changed in the reply after feedback (checked against the critique record); MU:Cr3.2.1a, a guide look-for: the finished reply played and its purpose said; MU:Pr5.1.1a, a guide look-for: the point named in the group's feedback is better than the record sheet's rehearsal mark; MU:Pr5.1.1b, a table item: the child shows the strategy on the song's hard spot; MU:Pr6.1.1a, a guide look-for: sung expressively for the song's purpose; MU:Pr6.1.1b, a guide look-for: the group's plan card names its audience and purpose, and the performance matches it. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: set criteria and the child's own choice of strategy (grade 2). Tools: the practice card, the concert plan card, the critique card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the class's songs, found instruments. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 1 because the concert is built from the year's songs and replies and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the unit's check day, the class concert in groups, is its own end-of-unit assessment | Candidate: a doubled version of every group part for the home room | Mariachi: the sensitivity agent reads the group's page and a museum's or a cultural body's page on mariachi (on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2011) first; no costume (no sombreros or charro dress), no accents, and no child is asked to speak for a culture. Los pollitos dicen is sung in Spanish from a read-aloud guide; a Spanish-speaking child may lead it by choice only. Groups only, no solo; performing rules as unit 4. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 2 | Bright or dark, twos or threes. *How can we tell a song's mood and its swing, and what do they do for the music?* | 16 | mu-g2-read; mu-g2-respond | MU:Pr4.2.2a; MU:Pr4.2.2b; MU:Re7.2.2a; MU:Re9.1.2a; MU:Cn11.1.2a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re9); connecting (Cn11). Works: "Arirang" (Korea, a folk song in threes, on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2012), sung by the class; "Tumbalalaika" (an Eastern European Jewish folk song in Yiddish, traditional), minor and in threes, in an open recording or sung by the teacher; Scott Joplin, "The Entertainer" (United States, 1902, public domain), major and in twos, in a checked recording | Retrieval in day 2's opener: picture rhythms and melody direction (mu-g1-read) and the purpose of beat and pitch (mu-g1-respond). MU:Pr4.2.2a: show knowledge of tonality (major, "bright", or minor, "dark") and meter (twos or threes, shown by a conducting pattern) in songs of more than one culture chosen for performance (Arirang and one other). MU:Pr4.2.2b: read and perform rhythm and melody patterns from picture or standard notation: ta, ti-ti and a rest, and so, mi and la on a three-line staff; counted by beats and a spoken counting system, with no note values as parts of a whole (lesson 13). MU:Re7.2.2a: describe how a music concept serves a purpose (ragtime's bouncing beat for dancing; a slow minor tune for missing someone). MU:Re9.1.2a: judge music for a purpose by taste and expression. MU:Cn11.1.2a: show how a piece relates to another art, a school subject, a setting or daily life (Arirang and a mountain pass on a map; ragtime and a dance). Words: major, minor, meter, rest, staff, mood. Showing: Arirang sung with a read rhythm accompaniment on found percussion for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.2.2a, a table item: for two unseen short pieces, the child conducts twos or threes and holds up the bright or dark card; MU:Pr4.2.2b, a performance item: the child reads and plays a four-beat rhythm card and sings a three-note melody card; MU:Re7.2.2a, a talk item: how one concept in an unseen piece suits its purpose; MU:Re9.1.2a, a kept listening-log page made on the last making day: two unseen pieces judged for a named purpose, the pick circled with a written or dictated reason; MU:Cn11.1.2a, a talk item: what a piece goes with (an art, a subject, a place or a time of day) and why. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: song form and note values (grade 3). Tools: rhythm and melody cards, the three-line staff card, conducting picture cards, work cards, Curio Class's own; a notation reader on the teacher screen or the printed cards. Free path: found percussion, the teacher sings. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 2 because it reopens reading after the summer and adds melody reading and naming tonality and meter, which unit 11's patterns inside a given tonality and meter need. The rhythm-syllables and describe-interpret-judge models are retrieved here | Candidate: a piece in threes or in minor from Africa or the Americas, in an open recording, for range | Arirang: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Korean cultural body's page first; the unit sings a common version and keeps to the song (some versions are tied to the division of Korea, not taught here). Tumbalalaika: the agent reads a Jewish music archive's page first; it is a secular riddle song; no costume, no accents. Joplin: his life is told plainly from a museum's or a library's published pages. Hazards and controls: found percussion as unit 5; conducting in place with room for arms. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | 2 | How performers show meaning. *How do music makers plan sounds to show what they mean, and how do we explain the music we pick?* | 16 | mu-g2-express; mu-g2-choose | MU:Pr4.3.2a; MU:Re8.1.2a; MU:Pr4.1.2a; MU:Re7.1.2a; MU:Cn10.1.2a | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re7, Re8); connecting (Cn10). Works: Maurice Ravel, BolAcro (France, 1928), one tune that grows from very soft to very loud as instruments join, its rights line (score and recording) checked in the unit plan; Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, Twelve Variations on "Ah vous dirai-je, Maman" (Austria, about 1781), one tune shown many ways, a public domain score in a checked recording; Joanne Shenandoah (Oneida Nation, Haudenosaunee, 1957 to 2021), a song of her own writing, described and linked to her estate's or a museum's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: loudness and speed named (mu-g1-express) and a pick for a purpose (mu-g1-choose). MU:Pr4.3.2a: show how creators use loudness, speed and other qualities (smooth or short sounds) to express what they mean, by performing a song with planned parts on the expression map. MU:Re8.1.2a: show how music concepts serve what creators and performers intend, by listening to two performers of one tune and saying how each one's choices show its meaning. MU:Pr4.1.2a: show and explain interest in, knowledge of and purpose for varied music (a pick from a menu for a class purpose). MU:Re7.1.2a: explain how the child's interests and experiences shape a music choice for a purpose. MU:Cn10.1.2a: show how what the child likes, knows or can do shaped the group's expression plan, with a public or story-character option marked the same. Words: tempo, dynamics, smooth, short, intent, explain. Showing: each group performs its mapped song for a partner class and explains its plan. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.3.2a, a guide look-for on the showing: the planned parts performed as mapped; MU:Re8.1.2a, a talk item: two unseen Curio Class original takes of one tune, how each shows a different meaning; MU:Pr4.1.2a, a kept listening-log page made on the last making day: the menu pick for a class purpose with a written or dictated reason that names what the child knows about the piece; MU:Re7.1.2a, a talk item: how something the child likes or has done (or a story character's) shaped the pick; MU:Cn10.1.2a, a kept card: the group's expression map with the child's line "we planned ___ because ___". Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: comparing several performers' readings (grade 3), tone color named (grade 4). Tools: the expression map, menu cards, listening-log pages, Curio Class's own. Free path: the teacher sings the two takes. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 9 because naming major, minor and meter gives the words for explaining a pick; before unit 11 because patterns improvised for a purpose use the expressive plan made here | Candidate: a second living maker from Asia or Africa who performs one tune in several ways, described and linked | Shenandoah: the sensitivity agent reads her estate's or a museum's page and an Oneida Nation source first; the song is her own composition, not a ceremonial song; the class listens only. Choices: a public or story-character option beside the child's own, marked the same; no child explains a family's music. Hazards and controls: BolAcro's loud end at the set low level, with a quiet rest after; voice rules as unit 1. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 11 | 2 | Patterns into a piece. *How do musicians build a piece from patterns, and how do we put our patterns in order?* | 14 | mu-g2-invent; mu-g2-document | MU:Cr1.1.2a; MU:Cr1.1.2b; MU:Cr2.1.2a; MU:Cr2.1.2b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Steve Reich, Clapping Music (United States, 1972; born 1936, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), two clappers and one pattern that shifts, described and linked to the composer's or publisher's page; Babatunde Olatunji (Nigeria and the United States, 1927 to 2003), Drums of Passion (1959), patterns that lock together, described and linked to a museum's or the label's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: reply phrases in twos and threes (mu-g1-invent) and picture notation (mu-g1-document). MU:Cr1.1.2a: improvise rhythm and melody patterns for a given purpose (a "rain" pattern, an "alarm clock" pattern). MU:Cr1.1.2b: make patterns and ideas inside a tonality and meter the teacher gives (so, mi and la in threes or in twos). MU:Cr2.1.2a: show and explain why the patterns chosen express what the child means. MU:Cr2.1.2b: document, combine and sequence the ideas in picture or standard notation or a sound-only recording (an A-B piece: two patterns, in order, with a start and an end). Words: improvise, pattern, combine, order, piece. Showing: A-B pieces played by partners for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Cr1.1.2a, a performance item: the child improvises a pattern for a purpose card; MU:Cr1.1.2b, a performance item: two patterns inside the teacher's given meter and tonality; MU:Cr2.1.2a, a talk item: why these patterns show the purpose; MU:Cr2.1.2b, a kept card made on the last making day: the A-B piece in picture or standard notation, combined and in order, scored with the showing. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: the child choosing the tonality or meter, and a piece with a stated purpose and setting (grade 3). Tools: purpose cards, pattern cards, the three-line staff card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps and found drums; notation in crayon. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 9, whose melody reading and named tonality and meter a pattern inside a given tonality needs, and unit 10, whose expressive plan the purpose card asks for; before unit 12 because the concert revises this A-B piece | Candidate: a pattern-built piece by a living maker from Asia or Latin America, described and linked | Olatunji: the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or archive's page and his own published words first; the class makes its own patterns and does not copy a named drum tradition's patterns as its own. Hazards and controls: found drums (buckets, boxes) played with the hands at a hand-pat level, hearing protection or a quiet seat allowed; clapping kept soft; partners sit an arm's length apart. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
