Each check day: the class draws in its listening logs with no instruments while the teacher hears six groups of five
for about four minutes each; set-up and clean-up fit the rest of the 40 minutes. Every child's evidence is collected
once in a window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are band course days (year 1 starts on day 65, year 2 on
day 129).

## Cross-subject

No music row of grades K to 2 carries a cross_subject lean (data/progressions/arts.csv), so this course waits on no
other course and no other course waits on it. STANDARD.md 6's music and math lean (note values as fractions) starts in
grade 3 (mu-g3-read, math:3.NF.A.1). The contacts below are practiced, not owned, each with its "if later" line; all
are core courses (lesson 32), except the physical education boundary, which is a boundary, not a lean.

### Mathematics, kindergarten to grade 2 (same year)

| Used here | Contact | If math has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Unit 1 (year K): eight beats patted and counted | Kindergarten math counts objects one to one in its unit 1 (days 1 to 18) | The music unit counts beats aloud with the teacher only to four, and the beat strip shows hearts to point at; no counting is scored |
| Units 7 and 9 (years 1 and 2): twos and threes | Counting to 3 is kindergarten counting | Meter is felt and shown by a conducting pattern; no number work beyond saying "1, 2" or "1, 2, 3" |

No unit asks for note values as parts of a whole, fractions, measuring or adding beats (lesson 13).

### English language arts, kindergarten to grade 2 (same year)

Talk about works and the critique practice collaborative conversation (SL.K.1, SL.1.1, SL.2.1): practiced, not
owned. If ELA has not yet taught the turn-taking rules, the music unit's critique card shows them in pictures (listen,
one at a time, then answer) and the teacher models them on the first critique day.

### Physical education, grades K to 2 (a boundary)

Physical education grade 1 has a dance-to-the-beat unit (its unit 13) and kindergarten units of moves in place. Here
children move only to show a music idea (the beat, a tune's shape, loud and soft) by stepping in place, patting and
tracing with the hands; the motor skill is PE's, and choreography is dance's (STANDARD.md 6; the dance K to 2 frame,
docs/courses/dance-grades-k-to-2/frame.md, moves to a steady beat and to two- and three-beat music). Music units
here hear and name the beat and meter; dancing them is dance's. No lean either way.

