"mu","K","mu-k-perform","MU:Pr5.1.Ka; MU:Pr5.1.Kb; MU:Pr6.1.Ka; MU:Pr6.1.Kb","","mu-g1-perform","","practice-plan","practice; again; feedback; expressive; audience; sing","","Use help from self, teacher and peers to improve a performance, try suggested ways to practice, and perform expressively for an audience such as the class. What changes: first year: a performance is improved with help and given to an audience such as the class. Assessed by a performance check: the teacher marks each child's performance as met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: volume stays low, no child is made to sing alone, and a group or recorded version is always an option."
"mu","K","mu-k-choose","MU:Pr4.1.Ka; MU:Re7.1.Ka; MU:Cn10.1.Ka","","mu-g1-choose","","listening-log","favorite; like; choose; why; song; interest","","Bring a song the child likes (or pick one from the class list), tell the class what is good about it, and tell how what the child likes or knows shaped a choice (a song, or a way to sing it), with a story character's choice as an option marked the same. What changes: first year: what interests a child is shown and said. Assessed by a favorites check: the teacher marks each child's show-and-say as met, partly or not yet."
"mu","K","mu-k-respond","MU:Re7.2.Ka; MU:Re9.1.Ka; MU:Cn11.1.Ka","","mu-g1-respond","","describe-interpret-judge","beat; tune; like; judge; art; school","","Move to the beat or show the tune going up and down while a piece plays, say whether the child likes it, and name a story, picture or game the music goes with. What changes: first year: a music idea is shown and a taste is stated. Assessed by a listen-and-say check: the teacher marks each child's answers as met, partly or not yet."
"mu","1","mu-g1-invent","MU:Cr1.1.1a; MU:Cr1.1.1b","mu-k-invent","mu-g2-invent","idea-bank","idea-bank","reply; phrase; purpose; major; minor; twos and threes","","Make a reply phrase for a purpose such as a greeting or a goodnight, and try ideas that sound happy or sad and that move in twos or threes. What changes: a purpose for each idea and a first try at different tonalities and meters. Assessed by a music-making check: the teacher marks each child's phrases as met, partly or not yet."
"mu","1","mu-g1-document","MU:Cr2.1.1a; MU:Cr2.1.1b","mu-k-document","mu-g2-document","notation-ladder","notation-ladder","choose; reason; notation; recording; picture symbols; express","","Play ideas chosen to express an intent, say why they were chosen, and document them in picture or standard notation or in a recording. What changes: the child gives a reason and documents the idea. Assessed by a kept-ideas check: the teacher marks the reason and the record as met, partly or not yet."
"mu","1","mu-g1-revise","MU:Cr3.1.1a; MU:Cr3.2.1a","mu-k-revise","mu-g2-revise","critique-protocol","critique-protocol","feedback; polish; purpose; meaning; share; change","","Play or sing a short idea for a partner and the teacher, change one thing after their feedback, then share the finished music and say what it is for. What changes: the child polishes with less help and says what the music is for. Assessed by a sharing check: the teacher marks the change and the purpose as met, partly or not yet."
"mu","1","mu-g1-read","MU:Pr4.2.1a; MU:Pr4.2.1b","mu-k-read","mu-g2-read","rhythm-syllables","rhythm-syllables","beat; melody direction; picture notation; rhythm; culture; pattern","","Show the beat and which way a tune moves in music from many cultures, and read and perform short rhythm patterns in picture or standard notation. Beat counts and picture symbols only, no note values. What changes: reading begins with simple rhythm patterns from picture symbols. Assessed by a read-and-play task scored on a three-line guide. Teacher note: music from a culture is chosen from that community's own published teaching or a named practitioner. A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"mu","1","mu-g1-express","MU:Pr4.3.1a; MU:Re8.1.1a","mu-k-express","mu-g2-express","expression-map","expression-map","loudness; speed; express; creator; performer; show","","Sing one song in two ways (loud and soft, fast and slow) and tell which fits what the song means, then name the choice a recorded performer made. What changes: the child names how loudness and speed carry what a creator means, where kindergarten only noticed them. Assessed by a listen-and-show check scored on a three-line guide."
"mu","1","mu-g1-perform","MU:Pr5.1.1a; MU:Pr5.1.1b; MU:Pr6.1.1a; MU:Pr6.1.1b","mu-k-perform","mu-g2-perform","practice-plan","practice-plan","rehearse; strategy; expressive; purpose; audience; feedback","","Use feedback from self, teacher and peers to improve a performance, try a suggested rehearsal strategy on a hard spot, and perform expressively in a way that fits both the audience and the purpose. What changes: less help, and a performance fitted to an audience and a purpose. Assessed by a performance check: the teacher marks each child's performance as met, partly or not yet."
"mu","1","mu-g1-choose","MU:Pr4.1.1a; MU:Re7.1.1a; MU:Cn10.1.1a","mu-k-choose","mu-g2-choose","listening-log","listening-log","interest; purpose; experience; choose; skill; knowledge","","Pick a piece from a short listening menu for a purpose such as a lullaby or a march, tell why, and tell how what the child likes, knows or can do shaped the pick, with a public or story-character option marked the same. What changes: choices are tied to a purpose for the music, where kindergarten only showed interest. Assessed by a favorites check with one spoken reason, marked met, partly or not yet."
"mu","1","mu-g1-respond","MU:Re7.2.1a; MU:Re9.1.1a; MU:Cn11.1.1a","mu-k-respond","mu-g2-respond","describe-interpret-judge","describe-interpret-judge","beat; pitch; purpose; style; judge; connect","","Listen to pieces in different styles and show with the body where the beat or the pitch does its job, say which the child likes for a given purpose, and link one piece to a story, a picture or a school subject. What changes: the child names a purpose that beat or pitch serves, where kindergarten only showed an idea. Assessed by a listen-and-say check, marked met, partly or not yet. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"mu","2","mu-g2-invent","MU:Cr1.1.2a; MU:Cr1.1.2b","mu-g1-invent","mu-g3-invent","idea-bank","idea-bank","improvise; pattern; rhythm; melody; tonality; meter","","Improvise rhythm and melody patterns for a purpose, then make patterns inside a tonality and meter the teacher gives. What changes: improvising on purpose and working inside a given tonality and meter. Assessed by a performance of two patterns scored on a three-line guide."
"mu","2","mu-g2-document","MU:Cr2.1.2a; MU:Cr2.1.2b","mu-g1-document","mu-g3-document","notation-ladder","notation-ladder","combine; sequence; document; notation; recording; order","","Show and explain why patterns were chosen, then document, combine and sequence ideas in picture or standard notation or a recording. What changes: ideas are combined and put in order, not only kept one by one. Assessed by a documented short piece scored on a three-line guide."
"mu","2","mu-g2-revise","MU:Cr3.1.2a; MU:Cr3.2.2a","mu-g1-revise","mu-g3-revise","critique-protocol","critique-protocol","feedback; revise; sense; mean; for; share","","Play a short piece for self, partner and teacher feedback, choose which advice to use and revise, then share the finished music and tell what it means and what it is for. What changes: the child decides which feedback to use. Assessed by a revised piece and a one-line note on the change, scored on a three-line guide."
"mu","2","mu-g2-read","MU:Pr4.2.2a; MU:Pr4.2.2b","mu-g1-read","mu-g3-read","rhythm-syllables","rhythm-syllables","tonality; meter; major; minor; rhythm pattern; melody pattern","","Learn songs from more than one culture and show whether each is major or minor and moves in twos or threes, then read and play rhythm and melody patterns from picture or standard notation. Rhythms are taught by beat counts and a spoken counting system. What changes: tonality and meter are named, and melody patterns are read as well as rhythms. Assessed by a read-and-play task scored on a three-line guide. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"mu","2","mu-g2-express","MU:Pr4.3.2a; MU:Re8.1.2a","mu-g1-express","mu-g3-express","expression-map","expression-map","tempo; dynamics; concept; intent; creator; performer","","Perform a song with planned loud, soft, fast and slow parts, then listen to two performers and say how each one's choices show what the music means. What changes: qualities beyond loudness and speed are tied to a creator's intent. Assessed by a performance and a spoken reason scored on a three-line guide."
"mu","2","mu-g2-perform","MU:Pr5.1.2a; MU:Pr5.1.2b; MU:Pr6.1.2a; MU:Pr6.1.2b","mu-g1-perform","mu-g3-perform","practice-plan","practice-plan","criteria; accurate; expressive; effective; strategy; rehearse","","Score a class performance with a short checklist, practice one hard spot with a named strategy such as slow, then up to speed, and perform for a purpose such as a morning song. What changes: the child chooses the strategy and judges with set criteria. Assessed by a performance scored on a three-line guide (accurate, expressive, fits the purpose)."
"mu","2","mu-g2-choose","MU:Pr4.1.2a; MU:Re7.1.2a; MU:Cn10.1.2a","mu-g1-choose","mu-g3-choose","listening-log","listening-log","interest; purpose; explain; choose; knowledge; varied","","Choose music for a class purpose from a listening menu and explain the pick, and tell how something the child likes or has done shapes the choice. What changes: the child explains a choice in words, where grade 1 showed and discussed it. Assessed by a short spoken or written reason scored on a three-line guide."
"mu","2","mu-g2-respond","MU:Re7.2.2a; MU:Re9.1.2a; MU:Cn11.1.2a","mu-g1-respond","mu-g3-respond","describe-interpret-judge","describe-interpret-judge","concept; purpose; judge; taste; connect; subject","","Listen for how a piece's beat, tune or speed suits its purpose, judge it for that purpose by taste and expression, and connect it to another art or a school subject. What changes: the child describes how a music concept serves a purpose, where grade 1 only showed it. Assessed by a short listen-and-describe response scored on a three-line guide. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"mu","3","mu-g3-invent","MU:Cr1.1.3a; MU:Cr1.1.3b","mu-g2-invent","mu-g4-invent","idea-bank","idea-bank","improvise; setting; purpose; choose; fits; tonality","","Improvise rhythm and melody ideas, say how they fit a purpose and a setting, then invent ideas in a tonality or meter the child chooses. What changes: the child explains the fit and chooses the tonality or meter. Assessed by a performance and a spoken reason scored on a short rubric."
"mu","3","mu-g3-document","MU:Cr2.1.3a; MU:Cr2.1.3b","mu-g2-document","mu-g4-document","notation-ladder","notation-ladder","piece; purpose; rhythm; melody; standard notation; picture notation","","Make up a short piece for a purpose (a game song, a goodnight tune) and write its rhythm and melody in picture or standard notation, or record it. What changes: ideas become a piece with a stated purpose. Assessed by a documented piece, played with its purpose said, scored on a short rubric."
"mu","3","mu-g3-revise","MU:Cr3.1.3a; MU:Cr3.2.3a","mu-g2-revise","mu-g4-revise","critique-protocol","critique-protocol","criteria; judge; record; changes; audience; intent","","Improve a class piece with a checklist the teacher and class set, mark the changes on the page, and share it with an audience, saying how it shows what the child meant. What changes: class criteria guide the changes and an audience hears the result. Assessed by a revised piece with a recorded change list, shared with an audience with its intent described, scored on a short rubric."
"mu","3","mu-g3-read","MU:Pr4.2.3a; MU:Pr4.2.3b; MU:Pr4.2.3c","mu-g2-read","mu-g4-read","rhythm-syllables","rhythm-syllables","structure; section; rhythm; short tune; notation; context","math:3.NF.A.1 (a note value is a part of a whole bar)","Map the form of a song the class will perform, read and play its rhythms and short tune from picture and standard notation, and talk about how where and why people sing it shapes the performance. Note values are placed after the unit on fractions as parts of a whole. What changes: whole tunes are read in picture and standard notation, and the setting of a performance is described. Assessed by a form map, a read-and-play task and a written description of the setting, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit names it."
"mu","3","mu-g3-express","MU:Pr4.3.3a; MU:Re8.1.3a","mu-g2-express","mu-g4-express","expression-map","expression-map","reading; interpret; loudness; speed; intent; performer","","Try a song with different loudness and speed and pick the version that carries its meaning, then describe how a recorded performer uses the same tools. What changes: the child compares readings by different performers, where grade 2 named qualities in one. Assessed by a performance and a short description scored on a short rubric."
"mu","3","mu-g3-perform","MU:Pr5.1.3a; MU:Pr5.1.3b; MU:Pr6.1.3a; MU:Pr6.1.3b","mu-g2-perform","mu-g4-perform","practice-plan","practice-plan","ensemble; criteria; polish; manners; audience; venue","","Score a class ensemble take for accuracy with a shared checklist, rehearse the weak spots, and perform for an audience with the manners of a performer and a listener. What changes: ensemble work, class criteria and audience manners. Assessed by a performance, a take judged on class criteria and a self-check, with performer and audience manners observed, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: a solo or doubled version is always offered."
"mu","3","mu-g3-choose","MU:Pr4.1.3a; MU:Re7.1.3a; MU:Cn10.1.3a","mu-g2-choose","mu-g4-choose","listening-log","listening-log","setting; purpose; choose; reflect; knowledge; audience","","Choose a piece for the class to perform and present the reasons (what it is for, where it will be sung, what the class knows), and tell how it fits the student's own interests. What changes: setting joins interest and purpose as a reason for choosing music to perform. Assessed by a listening-log entry with a reason scored on a short rubric."
