docs/reviews\arts-words-read-2026-11-20.md:12:- The 26 reconstructed ids, read against t1 to t5 as well as stmts2.tsv: all state the real demand. MU:Cr2.1.Ka correctly folds two K statements into one line. The flag "MU Cn10.2" does not match anything: Cn10.1 and Cn11.1 are the music lines, and the source repeats one statement from K to 8, so identical lines there are correct. The same holds for MU:Pr6.1.1b and 2b, MU:Re9.1.Ka and Kb, MA:Cn10.1.1b and 2b (identical source statements).
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-10-s06-2026-11-17.md:6:6. [5, W.9-10.1c] The Meets sample ("The guidance excludes employers as helpers; therefore, a voter cannot choose an employer") is one sentence-level inference; it does not show words, phrases or clauses linking the major sections, which is what 1c asks | A student with no section-to-section links would still match the sample, so a wrong answer passes | Make the look-for "a link between two sections that names the relation" and use a sample such as "Having shown what the right allows, the next section turns to the two limits, which narrow, not cancel, that right." Keep the C contradiction as the Not yet and add a second Not yet with a link that names the wrong relation.
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-11-s01-2026-11-17.md:6:6. [field 5, A1 key and Does not] A1's Meets has the ending explicitly establish only "Brently returns and Louise dies", but the last sentence states a cause: "When the doctors came they said she had died of heart disease--of the joy that kills." The key then says "neither reading establishes a medical cause", and Does not reads "she definitely dies happy because the doctors say so" (the doctors say heart disease and joy, not that she was happy) | the explicit-meaning part of RL.11-12.1 is mis-keyed and the Does not sample is not a plausible wrong answer to the item as asked | Meets: add "the doctors report heart disease, 'the joy that kills'; that is their report, not proof of why she died". Does not: "the doctors say joy killed her, so the joy of seeing her husband is established (A)".
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-11-s05-2026-11-17.md:8:4. [Daily exits, rows 82 and 88, error D] D is "an inference is an explicit fact", but row 82's wrong answer ("the songs explicitly establish that every singer was happy") and row 88's ("he explicitly says his escape needed no planning") contradict the text (chapter II says slaves sing most when most unhappy; chapter XI's first paragraph says he planned his escape) | they are misreadings, not an inference promoted to fact, so D's repair (label a quotation, an inference and an unresolved matter) does not fix them, and the rule that a wrong answer is filed only under a letter whose text describes it is broken | Rewrite each as an overreach the text only suggests (row 82: "Douglass proves that everyone who called the songs happy knew they were sorrowful"; row 88: "He explicitly names everyone who helped him escape"), or give those rows no letter and mark the evidence missing.
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-3-s01-2026-11-17.md:4:4. [Field 5 closing paragraph, RF.3.3a and RF.3.3d word record, day 30] The list said to be "new-to-that-child" contains said and does (read on day 2 in the e2-tricky-spellings retrieval), helpful (read on day 2 and a week 7 vocabulary word) and careless (taught on day 7 with care and careful, and a week 7 vocabulary word); unhappy and redo come straight from the weeks 3 and 4 teaching words | The evidence tests remembered words, not the skill, and the plan says the words are reserved while its own days teach them | Build the list only from words no day names: for RF.3.3d irregular words not on any week list or retrieval (for example enough, laugh, through), and for RF.3.3a unseen bases with taught affixes (for example unlock, refill, hopeful, dislike) with the meaning question on two of them; add the reserved words to the Reserve sentence.
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-2-s04-2026-11-17.md:16:12. [5, items I2, I3] I2's Does-not sample ("One says soil and one says sunset") gives differences only, which is E (omits a similarity), but is filed under F; I3's error list names F only while its Does-not sample is filed under D | a wrong answer is filed only under the letter whose text describes it | I2: change the letter to E; I3: add D to the wrong answers or change the sample to a minor-detail answer ("Q has the word flowers and R has the word leaves") filed under F
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-9-s04-2026-11-17.md:7:4. [Field 5 teaching table, E67] The reserved "final arrival paragraph" is the same promised-versus-actual arrival contrast that the field 2 hook ("anticipated freedom beside actual arrival") and T67 ("what changes between the promised journey and school arrival") use. The library text has exactly one such paragraph ("I had arrived in the wonderful land of rosy skies, but I was not happy"). | An exit should be a fresh stimulus; this one is the hook again, so a student can answer from the discussion. | Keep E67's grammar part ("exhausted" to "exhaustion") and change the reading part to a first-half paragraph the hook and T67 do not use, for example the reserved paragraph on eating "by formula" in "The Cutting of My Long Hair": "How does the routine of the first meal qualify the promise of the new world?" Answer: shared rules replace choice and comfort, so the promised happiness is qualified. Wrong: the promise holds unchanged (O). Name the paragraph as reserved and keep it out of 67's main work.
docs/reviews\claude-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-kindergarten-s02-2026-11-17.md:2:2. [field 5, day 63 S1, RL.K.3] The only answer is "actual manuscript details" and "Exact meets sample awaits manuscript"; the item also asks about 03 as a whole, which children mapped on days 59 to 62 and again on the class chart | There is no key a scorer can use, and a child can answer from the class story-map rather than from the section heard that day, so the check is not cold | Write the key shape now: character is the child who carries the parcel (an adult may also be named), not the parcel (G); setting names where the story happens as snow thickens and the indoor pause (H); a major event is a linked change such as the pause or the safe arrival, not snow or the parcel alone (I). Ask one of the three parts about the section first heard on day 63 and the other two about the whole, and say the exact sample is added when 03 is written.
docs/reviews\claude-lens-sources-english-language-arts-grade-3-s01-2026-11-17.md:1:1. [Field 10 and field 5 days 19, 24; text g3-u1-06] The 1938 caption is never quoted; the day 19 panel ("headquarters", weekly restocking, "careful packing"), the day 24 source card (Hindman, Knott County, horse and mule, weekly saddlebags) and article 06 are our wording, described as "from the verified caption"; I could not open the DocsTeach page (403), so none of these claims is checked | Day 19 and 24 items (RI.3.1, RI.3.2, RI.3.7, W.3.2) rest on our summary where the source's words are needed, and the plan cannot tell the caption's claims from ours | Quote the caption's exact words in field 10 (a short line), list which panel sentences come from it, name a second free source (National Archives catalog or Kentucky Historical Society) for the weekly-meeting and restocking facts, and mark anything else "ours, an inference".
docs/reviews\codex-write-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-12-s06-2026-11-16.md:4:2. [5, P3] The “Meets” sample contains one transition, “However,” between two simple subject-verb-object sentences; the key requires only one explicit relationship | It does not demonstrate varied transitions and syntax linking major sections under W.11-12.2c | Replace it with linked section excerpts demonstrating distinct transitions and sentence structures; make that variety necessary for full credit.
docs/reviews\codex-write-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-grade-3-s04-2026-11-16.md:13:7. [5, 6 and U3, mismatched labels] Day 154's bare topic “water” is not B's small-detail misconception; E127's “Neither has supply” is not D's conflicting dictionary meaning; E128's “unsourced learning” is not necessarily P's preference-as-learning error; E139's “unsupported reason” is not necessarily M's personal liking. | These labels can send students to unrelated repairs. | Supply concrete examples matching the existing letters: a minor detail as main idea, a conflicting familiar meaning of supply, a personal preference presented as research learning, and liking presented as an opinion's reason.
docs/reviews\codex-write-lens-assessment-english-language-arts-kindergarten-s02-2026-11-16.md:7:4. [5, S1 and R1] The keys remain “actual manuscript details” and “beginning, change and ending”; S1 explicitly lacks its meets sample | Teachers cannot distinguish accurate responses from plausible invented details | Supply concrete characters, settings, major events, a complete keyed retell and complete meets and does-not-meet samples checked against the finished texts.
docs/reviews\codex-write-lens-sources-english-language-arts-grade-12-s07-2026-11-16.md:1:1. [U3 E109; U6 selections] E109 requires Plessy’s procedural account of the petitioner’s claim, but the selected pages omit that account. | Students lack the passage needed to distinguish reported allegations from the Court’s reasoning. | Add the relevant [Statement of the Case, pages 538 to 539](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USREPORTS-163/pdf/USREPORTS-163-537.pdf), label it separately from the majority opinion, and identify the reserved exit paragraph.
docs/reviews\codex-write-lens-sources-english-language-arts-grade-3-s01-2026-11-16.md:3:1. [5, day 19 panel; 10, g3-u1-06] The panel presents protective packing as a historical fact derived from the [1938 caption](https://docsteach.org/document/pack-horse-librarians/), which documents replenishing saddlebags but says nothing about careful packing protecting books | An added inference is presented with the authority of the archival source | Delete that sentence, explicitly identify it as an inference, or cite a source supporting the protective-packing claim.
