# Arts performance-standard words: second read, 2026-11-20

File read: data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt (1,114 lines). Reader: a fresh second reader, not the writer.

## Verdict: Pass with changes

The lines are accurate, in the file's own words, and read well for teachers. No mapping shift was found. A handful of lines drift in meaning or sit close to the source's sentence shape. Estimated factual error rate: about 2 percent (3 drifts in about 150 lines read in full); closeness issues add about 4 more lines in the sample.

## What was checked

- Script over all 1,114 lines: every id has a statement (ids match one to one); none is over 14 words; no em dashes or en dashes; no company or product names; the header carries the source, licence, citation line and the "ours, not theirs" note. The longest word-for-word run shared with any source statement is 3 words (476 lines reach exactly 3, none more). Most are stock phrases ("interpret art by", "compare and explain").
- The 26 reconstructed ids, read against t1 to t5 as well as stmts2.tsv: all state the real demand. MU:Cr2.1.Ka correctly folds two K statements into one line. The flag "MU Cn10.2" does not match anything: Cn10.1 and Cn11.1 are the music lines, and the source repeats one statement from K to 8, so identical lines there are correct. The same holds for MU:Pr6.1.1b and 2b, MU:Re9.1.Ka and Kb, MA:Cn10.1.1b and 2b (identical source statements).
- Random sample, seed 2026, 25 per discipline (125 lines), all grades K to III represented. Ids: DA Cr2.1.1a Cr2.1.3a Cr3.1.4b Pr4.1.3a Pr4.1.IIIc Pr5.1.5c Pr5.1.6c Pr5.1.7c Pr5.1.IIc Pr5.1.IIIb Pr6.1.1a Pr6.1.4b Pr6.1.7b Pr6.1.IIb Re7.1.Ib Re8.1.Ka Re8.1.4a Re8.1.6a Re8.1.8a Re9.1.IIIa Cn10.1.2a Cn10.1.5a Cn10.1.6b Cn11.1.3a Cn11.1.8a. MA Cr1.1.K Cr1.1.2 Cr2.1.8 Cr3.1.Kb Cr3.1.2a Pr4.1.5 Pr5.1.Kb Pr5.1.1b Pr5.1.4b Pr5.1.6c Pr5.1.Ic Pr5.1.IIb Pr6.1.Ka Pr6.1.2b Pr6.1.8a Pr6.1.Ib Re7.1.2b Re7.1.5b Re8.1.6 Re9.1.1 Re9.1.7 Cn10.1.2b Cn10.1.3b Cn10.1.IIIa Cn11.1.2b. MU Cr1.1.3a Cr2.1.2b Cr2.1.4a Cr2.1.4b Cr2.1.7b Cr3.1.4a Pr4.2.5a Pr4.2.5b Pr4.2.7a Pr4.3.2a Pr4.3.8a Pr5.1.Ka Pr5.1.4a Pr5.1.8a Pr6.1.4a Pr6.1.4b Re7.1.Ka Re7.1.2a Re7.2.1a Re7.2.5a Re7.2.6a Re7.2.8b Re8.1.1a Re9.1.7a Cn10.1.1a. TH Cr1.1.4b Cr1.1.8c Cr2.1.8b Cr3.1.1c Cr3.1.3a Cr3.1.5a Pr4.1.IIIa Pr5.1.Kb Pr5.1.6a Pr5.1.8a Re7.1.5a Re9.1.2a Re9.1.2c Re9.1.6a Re9.1.7b Cn10.1.Ka Cn10.1.7a Cn11.1.Ka Cn11.1.1a Cn11.1.6a Cn11.2.2b Cn11.2.4a Cn11.2.5a Cn11.2.5b Cn11.2.IIIa. VA Cr1.1.2a Cr1.1.8a Cr2.2.2a Cr2.3.3a Cr3.1.6a Cr3.1.8a Pr4.1.3a Pr5.1.8a Pr6.1.6a Re7.1.2a Re7.1.3a Re7.1.Ia Re7.1.IIIa Re7.2.2a Re7.2.4a Re7.2.5a Re7.2.7a Re7.2.8a Re7.2.IIa Re8.1.1a Re8.1.8a Re9.1.8a Cn10.1.Ia Cn10.1.IIIa Cn11.1.5a.
- Mapping: a, b, c order and grade columns confirmed against the t files for the sample and the flagged ids (including the mislabeled music columns in t3, which stmts2.tsv resolves correctly).
- Progression, 12 threads read K to 8 and I to III: DA:Re8.1, MA:Cr3.1, MU:Pr6.1, TH:Cr3.1, VA:Cn11.1, VA:Re8.1, MU:Pr5.1, MU:Re8.1, MU:Re7.2, MU:Cr2.1, TH:Re8.1, DA:Cn10.1. The lines show what changes (mood, then media, then context; teacher criteria, then class criteria, then your own). Neighbouring lines that are identical in the file are identical in the source. The only near-twins with real differences underneath are listed in findings 4 and 5.

## Findings

1. Confidence: Likely. TH:Cr2.1.8b
   Current: Split leadership and duties to set shared aims while rehearsing or devising a play.
   Problem: the source says to share leadership; "split" changes the demand, and "Split ... set shared" echoes the sentence shape.
   Proposed: Take turns leading and sharing jobs to set group goals while rehearsing or devising.

2. Confidence: Likely. MU:Pr5.1.6a, MU:Pr5.1.7a, MU:Pr5.1.8a
   Current (8a): Use your own criteria to rehearse, polish, and judge whether music can be performed.
   Problem: the demand is to decide when the music is ready to perform, not whether it can be performed. Same slip on all three.
   Proposed 8a: Use your own criteria to rehearse, polish, and decide when music is ready.
   Proposed 7a: Use class-made criteria to rehearse, polish, and decide when music is ready.
   Proposed 6a: Use teacher criteria to rehearse, polish, and decide when a piece is ready.

3. Confidence: Likely. MA:Pr4.1.5
   Current: Build a media piece integrating several content types and forms, for example a broadcast.
   Problem: keeps the source clause order (integration of multiple contents and forms, such as broadcast).
   Proposed: Make a media piece that blends different kinds of content, like a newscast.

4. Confidence: Likely. MU:Cr3.2.1a and MU:Cr3.2.2a
   Current (1a): With little help, perform finished ideas for classmates or a casual audience.
   Problem: drops the purpose and expressive intent that both statements require; 2a says "expressing a purpose", which blurs intent and purpose.
   Proposed 1a: With little help, share finished music with classmates, showing your purpose and meaning.
   Proposed 2a: Share finished music with classmates, showing what it means and what it is for.

5. Confidence: Judgment call. VA:Re9.1.8a
   Current: Build a convincing, reasoned argument backing your judgment of an artwork.
   Problem: keeps "convincing" and the argument-for-an-evaluation frame.
   Proposed: Argue with solid reasons for your judgment of an artwork.

6. Confidence: Judgment call. VA:Re8.1.1a to VA:Re8.1.8a (nine lines)
   Current: all open "Interpret art by ...", the standard's own stock opener.
   Proposed opener, same thread (for example 1a): Work out an artwork's meaning by sorting subject matter and naming features of form.

7. Confidence: Judgment call. TH:Re9.1.7b
   Current: Think about the aesthetic qualities of a show's production elements.
   Problem: near mirror of "Consider the aesthetics of the production elements".
   Proposed: Notice how a show's sets, lights, sound, and costumes look and feel, and why.

8. Confidence: Judgment call. TH:Re9.1.3c
   Current: Judge the troubles and circumstances in a play as an audience member would.
   Problem: "troubles" is odd for a teacher; the demand is problems and situations.
   Proposed: Judge the problems and situations in a play as an audience member would.

9. Confidence: Judgment call. MA:Pr5.1.IIb
   Current: Show skilled adaptability, like resisting closure and learning from failure, on tough challenges.
   Problem: "resisting closure" is the source's own jargon, unclear to a teacher.
   Proposed: Keep exploring instead of settling early, and learn from failures on hard challenges.

10. Confidence: Judgment call. DA:Pr5.1.1a
    Current: Perform a range of travelling and in-place moves, body patterns, shapes, and directions.
    Problem: keeps the source's list in its order.
    Proposed: Show many ways to travel and stay in place, with varied shapes, patterns, and directions.

## Systematic or one-off

One-offs. No id shift, no column slip, no lines that belong to a neighbour. The one repeated pattern is findings 2 and 6, where a drifting or stock phrase was copied along a thread.

## Fixes applied (2026-11-20)

All ten findings applied in the words file. Finding 6: VA:Re8.1 lines reworded away from the stock opener; four (2a, 4a, 5a, 7a) were rewritten by hand to stay within 14 words. Finding 10: the dance line was shortened to 13 words.

