hears six groups of five for about four minutes each; set-up and clean-up fit the rest of the 40 minutes. Every
student's evidence is collected once in a window; no artifact is scored twice. Days above are band course days (year
4 starts on day 65, year 5 on day 129).

## Cross-subject

Three music rows of grades 3 to 5 lean on math and two on English language arts (data/progressions/arts.csv). All
are core courses (lesson 32). Each lean has its "if later" line.

### Mathematics, grades 3 to 5 (same year)

| Used here | Row and contact | Earliest unit of math that teaches it | If math has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit 4 (year 3, days 49 to 64 of the year, school weeks 25 to 32): note values as parts of a whole bar | mu-g3-read, math:3.NF.A.1 | math grade 3 unit 6 (days 92 to 114), weeks 19 to 23 | The unit reads rhythms by beat counts and syllables only (a quarter note one beat, two eighths one beat shared), as units 1 to 3 do, and names no note value as a fraction |
| Unit 8 (year 4, weeks 25 to 32): equal note values, two quarters in one half | mu-g4-read, math:4.NF.A.1 | math grade 4 unit 2 (days 9 to 25), weeks 2 to 5 | Taught long before; if a school runs math in another order, beats are counted only |
| Unit 12 (year 5, weeks 25 to 32): mixed note values added within a bar | mu-g5-read, math:5.NF.A.1 | math grade 5 unit 9 (days 119 to 134), weeks 24 to 27 | The unit counts in whole and half beats (an eighth is half a beat, a dotted quarter one and a half), which is like-denominator adding from math grade 4 unit 3 (4.NF.B.3), and names mixed values as unlike fractions only after math's unit 9 |

Before unit 4 each year, written rhythms are beat counts and syllables only; time signatures in year 4 are beats a
bar, not fractions; no unit asks for measuring, percent or scale (lesson 13).

