Retrieval in day 2's opener: setting as a reason for a pick (mu-g3-choose) and set criteria with the setting (mu-g3-respond). MU:Pr4.1.4a: choose the class's concert piece from the menu and show how interest, knowledge, setting and the class's own technical skill (can we hold a second part?) shape the pick. MU:Re7.1.4a: show and explain on a listening-log page how a chosen piece reflects interests, experiences, purposes and contexts. MU:Cn10.1.4a: show how the student's tastes, knowledge and skills shape a choice in music, or an invented or public person's, marked the same. MU:Re7.2.4a: describe how form, elements and the social or cultural setting shape the response (Dvořák writing in America, the oud in Iraqi music, a song sung in Fon). MU:Re9.1.4a: judge a piece by set criteria and explain whether it fits its setting. MU:Cn11.1.4a: show how music relates to another art, a school subject or daily life. Technique: listening for form and instrument families with a listening map; the concert piece learned by ear. Words: context, culture, technical skill, explain, criteria, instrument family. Showing: the chosen piece sung for a partner class with the class's argument for it. End-of-unit evidence, id by id: MU:Pr4.1.4a, a kept card: the pick and the reasons, one of them the class's skill; MU:Re7.1.4a, a listening-log page: how one chosen piece reflects a purpose or a context, explained; MU:Cn10.1.4a, a talk item: one taste, piece of knowledge or skill and the choice it shapes (own, invented or public, marked the same); MU:Re7.2.4a, a written item on an unseen open recording: how its form or an element and its setting shape the response, with a "because"; MU:Re9.1.4a, a written item on the same recording: set criteria marked and why it fits or does not fit its setting; MU:Cn11.1.4a, a talk item: one tie between an unseen piece and another art, subject or daily life. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: evidence cited from the music (grade 5). Tools: the listening map, the criteria card, the menu cards and the listening-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: the printed map. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions
First in grade 4 because it reopens the listening routine and the log after the summer and widens setting into culture, which units 6 and 8 use; the piece chosen here is unit 8's concert piece
Candidate: a check that each menu song's verses and history are clear before the class sings it
Dvořák's own claims about African American spirituals and Native American music as sources are told as his claims, with Harry T. Burleigh, who sang spirituals for him, named; the sensitivity agent reads a museum's or a scholarly page first. "Shenandoah": the agent checks its verses and the class sings a checked set. AlHaj left Iraq as a refugee: told in one plain sentence from his own page, read first by the agent, and no student is asked about their own family's travels. Own-taste tasks have an invented or public option. Hazards and controls: recordings at a set low level; voice rest. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on).

